`npm run typecheck-client` (the fork's client gate) could not pass on the tip.
Compiling the two crusorai preview files with TypeScript 5.8.2 reported five
diagnostics, four distinct sites:

  crusorAIPreviewService.ts(105) TS1109  new MutableDisposable<DisposableStore>>()
  crusorAIPreviewService.ts(158) TS2339  IEnvironmentService has no `userHome`
  crusorAIPreviewService.ts(324) TS2739  `listen` promised Disposable, returned IDisposable
  crusorAIPreview.ts(186/189)    TS2322  pairs assigned to `string[]`

Only the first is fatal-but-silent: `node --check` cannot see it, because in
plain JavaScript `a < b >> c` parses as comparison and the TS parser is the one
that knows `<...>` is a type argument list. The others are type-level only —
type erasure means the shipped runtime behaviour was correct (verified: the
tuple annotation type-erases into an array of pairs, and the window URL was
never actually malformed) — but every one of them fails the client gate.

`userHome` is on INativeEnvironmentService, which the workbench does not
register and which a browser-layer file must not reach for. The workbench way
to ask is IPathService.userHome({ preferLocal: true }) — synchronous from
AbstractPathService, and NativePathService is constructed with exactly the
environmentService.userHome URI this file wanted. It is also what chat,
search, tasks and debug already do.

Nothing pinned previewWindowQuery's output, so the contract test now round-trips
it through URLSearchParams: trimmed origin, reserved characters in the secret,
key order, and no key emitted for an absent value. 67 tests pass.

